---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { CONTACT } from '../../data/site';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Funerals we arrange', url: '/funeral-services/' },
  { name: 'Repatriation', url: '/funeral-services/repatriation/' },
];
---

<Page
  title="Repatriation — Bringing Someone Home"
  heading="Repatriation"
  description="Bringing someone home to East Lothian from elsewhere in the UK or from abroad, or arranging for them to travel onward. John Bald Funeral Directors, Dunbar."
  standfirst="Whether they need to come home to Dunbar, or to go somewhere else entirely."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <p>
    When someone dies away from home, the practical side becomes considerably more
    complicated at exactly the moment you have least capacity for it. We handle all of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Bringing someone home</h2>

  <h3>From elsewhere in the UK</h3>
  <p>
    Straightforward. We will liaise with the funeral director, hospital or coroner where
    the person died and bring them back to Dunbar, usually within a few days. There are
    no special documents needed for travel within the UK, though a death in England or
    Wales is registered under different rules from Scotland and we will explain what that
    means for you.
  </p>

  <h3>From abroad</h3>
  <p>
    More involved, and it takes longer — typically one to three weeks, depending on the
    country. We work with agents in most countries and will manage the whole process:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Registering the death in the country where it happened</li>
    <li>Obtaining the local death certificate and having it translated</li>
    <li>Arranging embalming, which most airlines require for repatriation</li>
    <li>A zinc-lined coffin, also usually required</li>
    <li>Consular paperwork and the certificate authorising removal</li>
    <li>Airline arrangements and customs clearance at this end</li>
    <li>Collecting the person from the airport and bringing them here</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout title="If you are dealing with this right now">
    <p>
      Telephone us before you agree anything with anyone abroad. Local funeral directors
      overseas sometimes quote very high prices to families in distress, and we can often
      arrange the same thing for considerably less through our own agents. There is no
      charge for that conversation.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3">
      If the person had travel insurance, check it first — repatriation is frequently
      covered, and the insurer may want to appoint their own agent.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h3>The Foreign Office</h3>
  <p>
    The FCDO can help with registering a death abroad and can put you in touch with local
    authorities, though they cannot pay any costs. If a death abroad has just been
    reported to you, they are usually the right first call after us.
  </p>

  <h2>Taking someone from here to elsewhere</h2>
  <p>
    We also arrange the journey the other way — for someone who has died here but whose
    family, faith or wishes mean they should be buried somewhere else, in another part of
    the UK or another country. The paperwork is different in each direction, and we
    handle it either way.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where a faith requires burial within a particular time, tell us at the very first
    call. It changes the order things need to happen in, and we will work to it.
  </p>

  <h2>What it costs</h2>
  <p>
    Repatriation costs depend almost entirely on the country and the airline, so we cannot
    put a single figure on this page honestly. What we will do is get you a full written
    breakdown before anything is committed to, showing what is our fee and what is being
    paid to third parties. We do not add a markup to third-party costs.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="accent">
    <p>
      Telephone{' '}
      <a href={CONTACT.phoneHref} class="font-semibold">{CONTACT.phone}</a> at any hour.
      If you are calling from abroad, the number is +44 1368 863636.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Page>
